<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guglielmo Marconi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guglielmo Marconi was an Italian inventor and engineer who developed practical long-distance radio telegraphy. He patented his system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1896 and in 1901 received the first transatlantic radio signal, sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>. He shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics in 1909 and from 1930 presided over the Royal Academy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> under the Fascist regime.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guglielmo-marconi.jpg" alt="Guglielmo Marconi" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pach Brothers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guglielmo_Marconi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Guglielmo Marconi was born on 25 April 1874 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, the son of Giuseppe Marconi, an Italian landowner, and his second wife, Annie Jameson, a granddaughter of the founder of Jameson Irish Whiskey. Between the ages of two and six he lived in Bedford, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He had no formal schooling and learned chemistry, mathematics and physics from private tutors. At 18 he met Augusto Righi, a professor of physics at the University of Bologna, who let him attend lectures and use the university's laboratory and library.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At 20 Marconi began experiments with radio waves, building much of his equipment in the attic of the Villa Griffone at Pontecchio. At Righi's suggestion he used a coherer, a detector that changed resistance when exposed to radio waves. In the summer of 1895 he found that raising the antenna and grounding the transmitter and receiver gave a much greater range, about 3.2 kilometres over hills. After a demonstration for the British government in July 1896 he sent Morse signals across Salisbury Plain, and on 13 May 1897 sent the first wireless message over open sea, across the Bristol Channel. He founded The Wireless Telegraph &amp; Signal Company, later the Marconi Company, in 1897.</p><p>The reported reception at Signal Hill in 1901 met scepticism then and since: the signals were faint, there was no independent confirmation, and the daytime path was a poor choice for the wavelength used. The sinking of the Titanic on 15 April 1912 showed the value of wireless and brought Marconi fame. He became an Italian senator in 1914 and a marquess in 1929, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-fascist-party">National Fascist Party</a> in 1923, and defended the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1935.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Marconi died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 20 July 1937, aged 63, after nine heart attacks in three years. At the hour of his funeral transmitters around the world observed two minutes of silence. His remains lie in a mausoleum at Sasso Marconi, beside his former villa, now a museum. On 21 June 1943 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-court-of-the-united-states">Supreme Court of the United States</a> held the broad claims of one of his patents invalid, because earlier patents of Oliver Lodge, John Stone Stone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-tesla">Nikola Tesla</a> anticipated them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guglielmo Marconi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally the period produced the operas of Giuseppe Verdi and Giacomo Puccini, the Futurist movement, <b>Guglielmo Marconi</b>’s radio and the educational method of Maria Montessori, while the racial laws of 1938 drove scientists such as Enrico Fermi into exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At Signal Hill in Newfoundland, <b>Guglielmo Marconi</b> heard the Morse letter 'S' sent from Poldhu in Cornwall, some 3,500 kilometres away.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-12">12 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.49,11.34&amp;year=1914" aria-label="Bologna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Guglielmo Marconi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5687 3739h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5687 3739h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.49,11.34&amp;year=1914">What was here in 1914? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
